          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining and ranking results from multiple retrieval methods in search systems.\\ntimestamp: 2026-01-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - concept/Search\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Two sources; one explicit description of hybrid retrieval in production RAG systems.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines sparse lexical search (e.g. BM25) with dense vector search, then merges the two ranked result sets to capture both exact term matches and semantic similarity. This approach consistently outperforms either method alone, particularly on queries that mix:\\n\\n- **Rare identifiers**: error codes, product SKUs, version numbers (where lexical precision is essential)\\n- **Natural language intent**: semantic queries that benefit from vector embeddings\\n\\n## Merging Strategies\\n\\nReciprocal rank fusion is a common technique for merging the ranked result sets from sparse and dense retrieval. It is robust across different scoring scales and has become a standard component of production RAG systems.\\n\\n## Production Adoption\\n\\nHybrid retrieval has become the default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems, replacing single-method approaches.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Hybrid retrieval for RAG systems (source: eval://update-over-duplicate/sample-01)\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
